WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.520
Where was your station physically on the campus?

2
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:10.420
In a small building right across from the Sigma Chi house.

3
00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:14.980
It was a cornfield when we . . . an experimental cornfield.

4
00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:24.300
And we had to wait until the Agriculture Department to finish their experiment with the corn before we could build . . . [laughs] start the building.

5
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:30.260
But there were some cracks made about that—where the station was being built.

6
00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:32.360
That it was appropriate to build it in a cornfield.

7
00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:37.220
I don’t . . . I think we lived up to a better reputation than that.

8
00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:45.260
Tell me exactly what the main part of the programming consisted of on that station.

9
00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:54.400
We had a varied program, but the greatest emphasis was on good music.

10
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:59.080
All kinds of good . . . no . . . we didn’t have any country music.

11
00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:02.220
Most of our program was music.

12
00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:06.460
But we did have other programming sources.

13
00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:15.940
We were a part of an educational radio network at that time that shipped discs,

14
00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:24.000
these fifteen-inch platters, from one station to another in sort of a round-robin fashion.

15
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.860
That was our network.

16
00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:32.260
After a while, a telephone wire network was established.

17
00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:35.300
But that was our beginning.

18
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:49.300
Then we developed a School of the Air in cooperation with East Baton Rouge Parish Schools where a number of subjects were taught.

19
00:01:49.300 --> 00:02:01.440
We started with music and then went into . . . we found sources of recordings . . . recorded programs in other fields.

20
00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:11.180
Western Electric, for example, put out a series of excellent science programs and sources like that.

21
00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.880
We had school programs.

22
00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:17.760
Then we developed various programs of our own.

23
00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:28.360
But during, I would say about a third of the day . . . weekdays, we’d broadcast to the schools of East Baton Rouge Parish.

24
00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:35.880
Of course that, too, was a problem because not many of them had FM radios.

25
00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:41.600
There was an FM presence in Baton Rouge when we started.

26
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:47.420
WJBO had established an FM station in addition to their AM.

27
00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:57.020
And it was a good music station for part of the day so that people sort of knew that there was such a thing.

28
00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:14.680
But a great deal of my time was spent visiting schools and PTA meetings and so on, telling them of the advantages of FM radio.

29
00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:20.440
Now what exactly was the sponsorship of and the financing of all this?

30
00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:22.360
Was it strictly an LSU affair?

31
00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:23.280
That’s right.

32
00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:23.620
That’s right.

33
00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:25.240
There was no state involved.

34
00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:31.300
No, no. No, the state was not involved other than through the LSU appropriation.

35
00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:32.900
Certainly not the Fed?

36
00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:37.800
No, no. It was purely an LSU project.

37
00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:42.960
Of course we had . . . It served an educational purpose on campus, too.

38
00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:55.920
Because our announcing staff . . . All of the announcers on the station were students who got the positions by auditions.

39
00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.640
All of our assistant engineers were students.

40
00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.120
Our chief engineer was not.

41
00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:10.400
But we started with a very small paid staff.

42
00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:17.340
Most of it was student work, but they were held to professional standards.

43
00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:28.180
All of the announcers who worked for us in our first year or two . . . first five years I would say . . . Particularly,

44
00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:32.520
I kept track of the ones that worked for us for the first two years.

45
00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:36.180
They all went into jobs at radio stations.

46
00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:37.720
I remember some of them.

47
00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:40.927
I remember one young man particularly by the name of Chuck Phillips.

48
00:04:40.927 --> 00:04:41.500
Yes.

49
00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:43.220
He was also from Iowa.

50
00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:44.200
Yes, right.

51
00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:46.148
He was here as a veteran.

52
00:04:46.148 --> 00:04:46.900
[agrees]

53
00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:53.640
And I later knew him as a regular radio staffer up in [?].

54
00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:55.360
There was a young man by the name of Campbell . . .

55
00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:55.800
Yes.

56
00:04:55.800 --> 00:05:00.540
. . . who became a fine major league play-by-play man for the Chicago White Sox.

57
00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:02.780
Yes, yes.

58
00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:09.240
Of course didn’t broadcast any sports, so they didn’t get their background or training in that area.

59
00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:15.280
But they learned to be at home with the microphone.

60
00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:24.140
They started out announcing programs of recorded classical music, most of them.

61
00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:30.720
It was a good training for them, because we had some good listeners who were helpful,

62
00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:45.220
in that if one of those announcers would mispronounce the name of a composer or a musical selection, the telephone rang.

63
00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:55.640
When the program was over, the telephone would ring and these helpful listeners would say, “I want to talk to the announcer.

64
00:05:55.640 --> 00:06:02.200
Did you realize that you mispronounced that composer’s name?”

65
00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:05.180
They got an education.

66
00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:06.140
It served two purposes.

67
00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:12.080
I could have told them the same thing, and it wouldn’t have had the effect.

68
00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:16.600
One thing, they knew that somebody had caught them in an error.

69
00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:29.400
But the most important thing was they knew they had a listener who cared and who was following them and was a fan of theirs.

70
00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:30.560
It was great education.
